      <h2>What results look like</h2>
      <p>Company pages publish bond-size figures. This site’s bond pages publish the premium bands used in the examples below.</p>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>Three company pages publish dollar figures a buyer can compare without inventing a premium. Travelers' contract page says federal construction projects valued at $150,000 or more require contract surety bonds. CNA Surety's commercial page says qualified corporate commercial programs run from $1,000,000 to $500,000,000. Old Republic's company page says performance and payment support goes to $100 million on a single project and $150 million on an aggregate program. Those are bond-size statements from the companies. They are not quotes.</p>
        <p>This site already publishes cost bands on its bond pages. The license and permit page lists a face range of $5,000 to $50,000, a premium band of 1% to 5%, and a typical annual premium of $100 to $500. The mortgage broker page lists a face range of $25,000 to $500,000, a premium band of 1% to 10%, and a typical annual premium of $500 to $5,000. The worked lines below use only those published bands. They are examples, not a rate from any company on this shortlist.</p>
        <p>Example, license and permit: a $10,000 face at the published 1% end of the band is $100 a year. The same $10,000 face at the published 5% end is $500 a year. That $500 figure is also the top of the page's typical annual range. Example, mortgage broker: a $25,000 face at the published 1% end is $250 a year. The same $25,000 face at the published 10% end is $2,500 a year, which sits inside the page's typical range of $500 to $5,000. A money transmitter example is not built here, because no company on this shortlist published a money transmitter bond.</p>
        <p>Put the company limit next to the face before you celebrate a rating. A $100 million single project, the Old Republic company-page cap, is above Old Republic Surety Company's Circular 570 per-bond limitation of $9,088,000. Treasury allows the excess when it is protected by reinsurance or coinsurance. Ask the agent which company signs, what the current Circular 570 limitation is, and how the excess is protected. The same question applies to a CNA corporate program at the $500,000,000 end of that published range, which is above Western Surety Company's $148,124,000 per-bond limitation.</p>
      </div>

